* k3s - minimal distro fo k8s:
   -- it's single binary
   -- k3s ships nearly all the userspace it needs (Kubernetes components, containerd, and the k3s-root bundle of iptables, conntrack, socat, etc., built with Buildroot) in one binary, so the host only needs a compatible kernel (namespaces, cgroups, netfilter) plus a minimal OS to launch it.

* k3d - a wrapper of k3s thar runs k3s in a container

* we have a lot of distors of k8s each one for a specific workload and use cases
* k8s has one standard implementation and other distros complient to it's behavior to be certified by cncf (https://www.cncf.io/training/certification/software-conformance/)


* kubenettes doesn't handle anything contairization or networking or storage it's has interfaces that plugs to it and work with them

* k3s builds all the binaries that it needs and embed them inside the binary using embed fs a golang feature


* vagrant used for creating vms (with the concept of Infrastructure as Code) so you define everything in a vagrant file and vagrant takes care of creating the vm
* vagrant have a concept of boxes which are the actual periconfigured vm the actual thing that vagrant runs
* and vagrant does run the actual boxes on it's own it needs a provider and (please check the box and which providers it can be run on)
* when vagrant vms are running vagrant adds an ssh tunnel to enter your vm
* to configure your vm to your actual need you can use vagratn previsioning (config.vm.provision)
